Add xwayland:scale, one scale for X11 clients on every monitor

X11 has one coordinate space and one DPI for all its clients, so an X11
window cannot take the scale of the monitor it is on. X11 apps either render
at 1 and are upscaled, which is blurry, or with force_zero_scaling render at
each monitor's pixels and need GDK_SCALE or Xft.dpi set by hand, which is
right on one monitor and wrong on the others.

xwayland:scale is the one scale every X11 client renders at: -1 is the
highest monitor scale rounded up to a whole number, -2 the highest monitor
scale as it is, or a number. 0, the default, changes nothing. Hyprland lays
the X11 screen out at that scale and scales X11 windows down to their
logical size itself, so a window is pixel for pixel on a monitor of that
scale, downscaled with linear filtering on a lower one, and never upscaled.
It needs nothing of Xwayland and changes no protocol. An X11 cursor is
shown the same way, at its size divided by the X11 scale of the window under
it, but not below Hyprland's cursor size. An app that draws its cursor at
the X11 scale (GTK, Qt) and one that takes XCURSOR_SIZE as it is (Chromium
and Electron apps, Steam, Wine) both get one the size of Hyprland's own, and
a cursor drawn smaller than that is shown at its own size, as today.

The scale is laid out with the monitors, so a config reload that changes
only xwayland:scale lays them out again, as a monitor change does.

The window rule xwayland_unscaled, and the switch xwayland:unscaled_windows,
keep an X11 window that draws at 1 whatever it is told (Steam's updater) at
its own size. Their members go last in their classes, so plugins built
against Hyprland's headers before this keep their layout.

From https://github.com/ParkerrDev/xscale.

